[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
## TL;DR  

1. **Upgrade to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0** (the version that understands an array of sprite sheets).  
2. **Add your sheet to the style’s `sprite` property** as a second entry (`id: "my-icons"`).  
3. **Reference the icons with the `id:` prefix** – e.g. `"icon-image": "my-icons:park"` .  
4. Make sure the URLs are **extension-less**, CORS-enabled, and that the sheet actually contains the image name you use.  

If you can’t upgrade, fall back to `map.addImage()` / `loadImage()` at runtime (or merge the sheets).  

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus a quick “debug checklist” you can run in the console.

---

## 1. Why the icons never appear

| What you have | What MapLibre expects |
|---------------|-----------------------|
| `sprite: "https://basemap.provider.com/sprites/basemap"` (string) | One sprite sheet only. All `icon-image` values are looked up **only** in that sheet. |
| `icon-image: "my-park"` (your POI name) | MapLibre searches the *basemap* sheet for `"my-park"` → **not found** → silently drops the icon (no error because the style loads fine). |

The style never knows about your second sheet, so the lookup fails. The engine does **not** warn about a missing image when the sprite JSON loads successfully – it only warns when the image ID is *referenced* but not present in any loaded sheet. Since the sheet you need isn’t loaded, the lookup never happens, and you see no warning.

---

## 2. The modern solution: **multiple sprite sheets** (MapLibre GL JS 3.0+)

Since v3.0 the `sprite` property can be **either** a string **or** an **array of objects**:

```json
{
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://basemap.provider.com/sprites/basemap" },
    { "id": "my-icons", "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi" }
  ],
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "cafes",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "places",
      "layout": { "icon-image": "my-icons:cafe" }
    }
  ]
}
```

### Key rules

| Rule | What it means |
|------|----------------|
| **`id` = `default`** | The sheet that gets *no* prefix. Keep the basemap sheet as `default` so all its existing `icon-image` values keep working unchanged. |
| **Prefix with `id:`** | Any image that lives in a non-default sheet must be referenced as `"my-icons:icon-name"`. |
| **All URLs are extension-less** | `url` must **not** contain `.json` or `.png`. MapLibre will automatically request `url.json`, `url.png`, and the `@2x` variants. |
| **Unique `id`s** | You cannot have two entries with the same `id`. |
| **CORS** | The sprite PNG/JSON must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your origin). Otherwise the sheet loads but the images are blocked. |
| **Version** | The array form is **not** understood by MapLibre GL JS < 3.0. Upgrade or you’ll get a “Invalid sprite URL” error. |

---

## 3. How to add the extra sheet

### 3-a. **Static style file (recommended)**  

Edit the style JSON (or the style you fetch from a server) and replace the single `sprite` string with the array shown above.  

If you generate the style on the fly (e.g. via a backend), just inject the array before you hand the style to the map.

### 3-b. **Programmatic addition after the style has loaded**  

If you cannot edit the source JSON (e.g. you receive a style from a third-party API), you can add the sheet at runtime:

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // Add the extra sheet – the first argument is the sheet id, the second the base URL.
  map.addSprite('my-icons', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi')
    .then(() => console.log('my-icons sprite sheet added'))
    .catch(err => console.error('Failed to add sprite sheet', err));
});
```

> **Note:** `addSprite` works *only* after the style’s original sprite(s) have finished loading. The promise resolves when the JSON and PNG are fetched and parsed.

### 3-c. **Fallback: `addImage`**  

If you are stuck on an older MapLibre version, you can load each POI icon manually:

```js
async function loadAndAdd(id, url) {
  const { data } = await map.loadImage(url); // PNG/WebP/JPEG only
  if (!map.hasImage(id)) map.addImage(id, data);
}

// Example
map.on('load', async () => {
  await loadAndAdd('cafe', 'https://mydomain.com/icons/cafe.png');
  // now you can use "cafe" directly in icon-image
});
```

*SVGs* need the `Image` element route (see the “Runtime images with `addImage`” section of the skill). This approach is fine for a handful of icons but becomes inefficient for large POI sets, because each icon is a separate HTTP request.

---

## 4. Full example (static style + prefix)

**style.json (excerpt)**

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/sprites/ofm_f384/ofm" },
    { "id": "my-icons", "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi" }
  ],
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "poi-cafes",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "places",
      "source-layer": "poi",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "my-icons:cafe",
        "icon-size": 0.8,
        "icon-allow-overlap": false
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**HTML / JS**

```html
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://mydomain.com/styles/my-style.json',
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],
    zoom: 13
  });

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // optional sanity-check
    console.log('Images available:', map.listImages().slice(0, 5));
  });
</script>
```

Now the POI layer will draw the `cafe` icon from **your** sheet, while all basemap icons keep working from the default sheet.

---

## 5. Debug checklist (run in the console after `map.on('load')`)

```js
// 1️⃣ Is the extra sheet loaded?
map.listImages().includes('my-icons:cafe'); // true → sheet is there

// 2️⃣ Does the style actually reference the prefixed name?
map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.id === 'poi-cafes').layout['icon-image']; // "my-icons:cafe"

// 3️⃣ Did the sheet load without CORS errors?
// Open DevTools → Network → filter “poi.json” / “poi.png” → status 200, no CORS block

// 4️⃣ If the image is missing, you’ll see a warning once:
map.on('styleimagemissing', e => console.warn('Missing image:', e.id));
```

If `listImages()` does **not** contain `my-icons:cafe`:

* Check the sprite JSON at `https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.json` – does it have a key `"cafe"`?  
* Verify the PNG (`poi.png`) contains the icon at the coordinates listed in the JSON.  
* Make sure the URL you gave in the style (`url` field) is **exactly** the base URL without any extension.

If the sheet fails to load at all, you’ll see a `error` event on the map:

```js
map.on('error', e => console.error('Map error:', e));
```

Typical causes: wrong URL, missing `.json`/`.png` (the style adds them automatically), or CORS headers missing.

---

## 6. What if you *cannot* change the style JSON?

1. **Add the sheet programmatically** with `map.addSprite('my-icons', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi')` (requires GL JS ≥ 3.0).  
2. **Load the icons individually** with `loadImage` + `addImage` (works on any version).  
3. **Merge the sheets** (last resort) – use `spritezero` or `spreet` to combine the PNG/JSON files into a single sheet and host that as the sole `sprite`.  

---

## 7. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Pitfall | Symptom | Fix |
|---------|---------|-----|
| **Forgot the `id:` prefix** | Icons silently disappear, no console warning. | Use `"my-icons:icon-name"` in `icon-image`. |
| **Used `url` with `.json` or `.png`** | Map tries to request `…/poi.json.json` → 404 → sheet never loads. | Remove the extension; give only the base URL. |
| **CORS header missing** | Sheet loads (JSON 200) but PNG is blocked → icons missing, `styleimagemissing` fires. | Serve PNG with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your origin). |
| **Running MapLibre GL JS 2.x** | `sprite` array is ignored → only default sheet used. | Upgrade to ≥ 3.0 or use `addImage`. |
| **Duplicate `id` in the array** | Style load fails with “all the sprites' ids must be unique”. | Ensure each entry’s `id` is unique (`default`, `my-icons`, …). |
| **Icon name typo** | `styleimagemissing` warning for the misspelled ID. | Double-check the key in the sprite JSON. |
| **Using an SVG with `loadImage`** | `loadImage` rejects the request. | Load the SVG via an `Image` element and pass that to `addImage`, or use `setMissingStyleImageResolver` (GL JS 6+). |

---

## 8. TL;DR code snippet (runtime addition)

If you prefer to keep the original style untouched and just inject your sheet after the map loads:

```js
map.on('style.load', async () => {
  try {
    await map.addSprite('my-icons', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi');
    console.log('✅ Added my-icons sprite sheet');
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('❌ Could not add sprite sheet', e);
  }

  // Example layer that uses the new sheet
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-poi',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'places',
    layout: {
      'icon-image': 'my-icons:park',
      'icon-size': 0.7
    }
  });
});
```

Now you have **zero merging**, **no rebuild**, and your POI icons appear alongside the basemap’s icons.

---

### Bottom line

*Put your own icons in a second sprite sheet, give that sheet an `

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste-ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain, rasterised into an `HTMLImageElement`, and registered with `map.addImage()`.  
* A GeoJSON source with ~5 000 points is added.  
* A **symbol layer** draws the icons (`icon-image`) from the `category` property.  
* Collision-detection (the default) keeps the icons from piling up when you are zoomed out.  
* A tiny bit of styling (`icon-size`, `icon-allow-overlap: false`) makes the result look tidy, and `symbol-sort-key` gives a deterministic “which icon survives” order.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link
    href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
    rel="stylesheet"
  />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Initialise the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any base style you like
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],   // San Francisco (change to your area)
  zoom: 12,
  hash: true
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Helper – load a single SVG as an Image element
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function loadSvgAsImage(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    // Important for cross-origin SVGs – otherwise addImage throws
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(new Error(`Failed to load ${url}`));
    img.src = url;
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  List of your 40+ icon names (without extension)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const ICONS = [
  // Example – replace with the real list you have
  'cafe', 'restaurant', 'park', 'museum', 'library',
  'hospital', 'school', 'bank', 'pharmacy', 'hotel',
  // … up to ~40 entries …
];

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Load every SVG, register it with the map, then add the data
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function registerIconsAndAddLayer() {
  // 4a – load all SVGs in parallel
  const loadPromises = ICONS.map(name =>
    loadSvgAsImage(`https://mydomain.example.com/icons/${name}.svg`).then(img => ({
      name,
      img
    }))
  );

  const loaded = await Promise.all(loadPromises);

  // 4b – add each image to the style (skip if it already exists)
  loaded.forEach(({name, img}) => {
    if (!map.hasImage(name)) {
      // `sdf: false` – we are not using a signed-distance-field icon
      map.addImage(name, img, { sdf: false });
    }
  });

  // 4c – add the GeoJSON source (replace the URL with yours)
  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: 'https://mydomain.example.com/data/venues.geojson'   // ~5 000 features
  });

  // 4d – add the symbol layer that uses the icons
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    // The `category` property must match one of the icon names above
    layout: {
      // Pull the icon name from the feature property
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],

      // Optional: make the icons a little smaller on the map
      'icon-size': 0.8,

      // Keep the default collision handling (no overlap)
      // `icon-allow-overlap: false` is the default, but we set it
      // explicitly for readability.
      'icon-allow-overlap': false,

      // When two icons collide, the one with the higher sort-key wins.
      // Here we simply use the feature's `id` (or any numeric field) so
      // the result is deterministic.
      'symbol-sort-key': ['coalesce', ['get', 'id'], 0]
    },
    paint: {
      // If you ever switch to SDF icons you could colour them here.
      // 'icon-color': '#ff6600',
    }
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  Wait for the style to load, then run the routine above
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('load', () => {
  registerIconsAndAddLayer()
    .catch(err => console.error('❌  Icon loading failed:', err));
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  Optional – listen for missing-image warnings (debug aid)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('styleimagemissing', e => {
  console.warn('Missing image:', e.id);
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does, step-by-step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Creates a `maplibregl.Map` with any base style. | Gives you a canvas to draw on. |
| **2️⃣ Helper `loadSvgAsImage`** | Returns a `Promise` that resolves to an `Image` element once the SVG has been fetched and rasterised. | `map.addImage()` only accepts raster images (PNG, JPEG, WebP, or an `HTMLImageElement`). |
| **3️⃣ Icon list** | An array of the 40 + category names that exist as `*.svg` files on your server. | Guarantees we only request icons we actually have. |
| **4a Load all SVGs** | `Promise.all` loads them in parallel → fast start-up. | No need to wait for each one sequentially. |
| **4b Register each image** | `map.addImage(name, img, {sdf:false})`. `hasImage` prevents duplicate-ID errors. | Makes the icons addressable by `icon-image`. |
| **4c Add the GeoJSON source** | Points with a `category` property (and optionally an `id`). | The data the layer will visualise. |
| **4d Add the symbol layer** | `icon-image` is an expression that reads the `category` property. Collision detection (`icon-allow-overlap:false`) keeps icons from piling up when zoomed out. `symbol-sort-key` gives deterministic conflict resolution. | The core of the visualisation. |
| **5️⃣ Run after `load`** | Guarantees the style’s sprite system is ready before we add our own images. | Prevents “style not loaded” errors. |
| **6️⃣ Debug listener** | Logs any missing-image warnings to the console. | Helpful when a `category` value does not match an icon name. |

### Tweaking the appearance

* **Icon size** – change `'icon-size': 0.8` to any number (1 = original SVG size).  
* **Zoom-dependent size** – use an expression, e.g. `'icon-size': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 10, 0.5, 14, 1]`.  
* **Overlap on very high zoom** – you can enable overlap only past a certain zoom level:  

  ```js
  'icon-allow-overlap': ['step', ['zoom'], false, 15, true]
  ```

* **SDF icons** – if you ever convert the SVGs to signed-distance-field PNGs you can set `{sdf:true}` and then colour them with `icon-color` / `icon-halo-*`.

That’s it! With the snippet above you’ll see each venue rendered with its proper SVG-derived icon, and MapLibre’s built-in collision handling will automatically hide icons that would otherwise overlap when you zoom out. Enjoy your map.

## TL;DR  

* **The current heuristic is not reliable.**  
  * `text-anchor` is *optional* for every symbol layer – it defaults to `"center"` when omitted, so both place-name layers **and** shield layers will have `undefined`.  
  * `symbol-placement` is often **not a plain string**. In the OSM Bright / OSM Liberty shield layers it is a **zoom-function object** (`{stops: …}`) that resolves to `"point"` at low zooms and `"line"` at higher zooms. Your test (`=== 'point'`) therefore fails for the real shield layers while still passing for many point-only POI layers that happen to have no `symbol-placement` set (the default is `"point"`).

* **What you really want to look for** is the *signature* of a shield layer – the way the style tells the renderer “draw a badge behind a road-ref”.  
  The most distinctive clues are:  

  1. **`icon-image` that is built from a network-/ref-length placeholder** (e.g. `"{network}_{ref_length}"` or an expression that concatenates `['get','network']` and `['_', ['get','ref_length']]`).  
  2. **`text-field` that references the road reference** (`"{ref}"` or `['get','ref']`).  
  3. **`icon-text-fit` set to `"both"` / `"width"`** (or the presence of `icon-text-fit-padding`). This is only used for stretchable shields.  
  4. **A sprite-sheet prefix** (`my-icons:us-interstate_2`, etc.) – the presence of a colon in the `icon-image` value is a strong indicator that the image lives in a *shield* sheet rather than a generic POI sheet.  
  5. **Layer source-layer name** – most OSM-based styles keep shield layers in a source-layer called `transportation_name` (or similar).  

  Anything that matches **(1) + (2)** is almost certainly a shield; anything that only matches **(1)** but not **(2)** could be a generic icon that happens to be named with an underscore (e.g. `cafe_24`).  

Below is a short, practical detection routine that you can drop into a style-audit script. It works with both the *string* and *array* sprite forms, and it tolerates the zoom-function object that the spec allows for `symbol-placement`.

```js
/**
 * Return true if a symbol layer looks like a route-shield layer.
 *
 * @param {object} layer – a layer object from a style JSON
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 1️⃣  The layer must have a text field that pulls the road reference.
  const hasRefText =
    typeof layout['text-field'] === 'string' && layout['text-field'].includes('{ref}') ||
    Array.isArray(layout['text-field']) && // expression form
      layout['text-field'].some(
        part => typeof part === 'string' && part.includes('ref')
      );

  if (!hasRefText) return false;

  // 2️⃣  The icon-image must be built from a network + ref_length placeholder.
  const iconImg = layout['icon-image'];
  if (!iconImg) return false;

  // Helper: does the value contain the placeholder pattern?
  const looksLikeShield = (val) => {
    if (typeof val === 'string') {
      // plain string: "{network}_{ref_length}" or "my-icons:us-interstate_2"
      return /\{?network\}?_?\{?ref[_-]?length\}?/.test(val) ||
             /(?:^|:)[a-z]+-[a-z]+_\d+$/.test(val); // e.g. "my-icons:us-interstate_2"
    }
    // expression form – look for a concat of get('network') and get('ref_length')
    if (Array.isArray(val) && val[0] === 'concat') {
      const parts = val.slice(1);
      const hasNetwork = parts.some(p => Array.isArray(p) && p[0] === 'get' && p[1] === 'network');
      const hasRefLen = parts.some(p => Array.isArray(p) && p[0] === 'get' && p[1] === 'ref_length');
      return hasNetwork && hasRefLen;
    }
    return false;
  };

  if (!looksLikeShield(iconImg)) return false;

  // 3️⃣  Optional but strong: icon-text-fit is used (stretchable badge)
  if (layout['icon-text-fit'] && layout['icon-text-fit'] !== 'none') return true;

  // 4️⃣  If we got this far we already have the two hallmark properties.
  //     Accept it as a shield – most styles don’t set icon-text-fit.
  return true;
}
```

### Why this works better than the original heuristic

| Heuristic | What it actually checks | Why it mis-fires |
|-----------|------------------------|-----------------|
| `layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined` | “no explicit anchor” | `text-anchor` defaults to `"center"` for **all** symbol layers, so the test is true for *any* point-only layer (place-names, POIs, etc.). |
| `layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'` | “placement is the literal string `point`” | Shield layers often use a **zoom-function object** (`{stops:[…]}`) that resolves to `"point"` at low zooms, so the strict equality fails. Plain POI layers that omit the property also default to `"point"` and therefore pass. |

The new heuristic looks at the *semantic* parts of a shield definition:

* **`text-field` referencing `{ref}`** – shields are the only layers that need the road reference as a label.  
* **`icon-image` built from `{network}_{ref_length}`** – that placeholder is unique to the OpenMapTiles shield implementation (and to most other shield sheets).  
* **`icon-text-fit`** – only stretchable shields set this; it’s a quick “yes-this-is-a-shield” flag.  

If you need to be even stricter (e.g. to avoid a custom icon named `cafe_2` from being mistaken for a shield), you can add a check that the **sprite sheet ID** (the part before the colon) is either `"default"` **or** a known shield sheet ID you have added via `addSprite`. For example:

```js
if (typeof iconImg === 'string' && iconImg.includes(':')) {
  const [sheetId, imgName] = iconImg.split(':');
  if (sheetId !== 'default' && !knownShieldSheets.includes(sheetId)) return false;
}
```

### Quick sanity-check against the OSM Bright / OSM Liberty styles

| Layer (OSM Bright) | `text-field` | `icon-image` | `icon-text-fit` | Detection result |
|---------------------|--------------|--------------|-----------------|------------------|
| `highway-shield-us-interstate` | `"{ref}"` | `"{network}_{ref_length}"` | *absent* | **true** (passes 1 + 2) |
| `highway-shield-us-highway`    | `"{ref}"` | `"{network}_{ref_length}"` | *absent* | **true** |
| `place-label-city`            | `"{name}"` | *none* | *none* | **false** |
| `poi-label-cafe`              | `"{name}"` | `"cafe"` | *none* | **false** |

The same logic works for the newer **stretchable** shields (e.g. `icon-text-fit: "both"`), because they also satisfy the first two conditions and additionally set `icon-text-fit`.

### Edge cases & how to handle them

| Situation | Why the simple test might fail | Recommended tweak |
|-----------|--------------------------------|-------------------|
| **Custom shield-like icons** (e.g. a “badge” layer that shows a logo but also has a `text-field` with `{name}`) | It may have `icon-image` that contains an underscore, but not the `{network}_{ref_length}` placeholder. | Keep the *placeholder* regex (`\{?network\}?_?\{?ref[_-]?length\}?`) – it won’t match arbitrary underscores. |
| **Shield layers that use a different placeholder** (some proprietary styles use `"{shield}"` instead of `{network}_{ref_length}`) | The regex won’t match. | Extend the detection to look for any `icon-image` that contains a **dynamic expression** referencing a numeric length field (`ref_length`, `ref_len`, etc.) – you can add those field names to an allow-list. |
| **Sprite sheets that are merged** (single sprite sheet that contains both POI icons and shields) | The `icon-image` may be a plain string like `"us-interstate_2"` without a colon. | The regex `/(?:^|:)[a-z]+-[a-z]+_\d+$/i` catches that pattern. |
| **Shield layers that deliberately hide the badge** (e.g. `icon-image: ""` to render only the number) | No `icon-image` → you’ll miss it, but that’s intentional – it’s not a *shield* in the visual sense. | If you need to flag “badge-missing” shields, you must also check the `network`/`ref_length` fields in the data source, not just the style. |

## Bottom line

* **Do not rely on `text-anchor` or a literal `'point'` comparison.** Those properties are either optional or can be expressed as a zoom-function object, so they are not discriminating.
* **Key off the *icon-image* placeholder and the *text-field* that pulls the road reference.** Those two together uniquely identify the classic route-shield pattern used by OSM Bright, OSM Liberty, and most OpenMapTiles-based styles.
* **Optionally, add a check for `icon-text-fit`** (or a known shield sprite-sheet prefix) to make the detection even tighter.

Replace the old heuristic with something like the `isShieldLayer` function above, and you’ll stop flagging ordinary place-name layers while correctly catching every real shield layer. Happy auditing!

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste-ready** example that shows how to

1. **Create a MapLibre GL JS map**  
2. **Add a draggable pin (Marker) at a known coordinate**  
3. **Show a Popup when the user clicks the pin**  
4. (Optional) keep the popup’s text in sync with the pin’s current location after a drag.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Draggable Pin + Popup – MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],             // lon, lat (San Francisco)
    zoom: 12
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Wait until the style is fully loaded before adding UI
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Create a Popup (but don’t open it yet)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
      offset: 25,               // distance from the marker tip
      closeButton: true,
      closeOnClick: false      // keep it open when the map is clicked
    }).setHTML('<strong>Drag me!</strong>');

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Create a draggable Marker at the desired coordinate
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
      draggable: true,          // <-- makes the pin draggable
      // You can also pass a custom HTML element here:
      // element: document.createElement('div')
    })
      .setLngLat([-122.42, 37.77])   // initial lon/lat
      .setPopup(popup)               // attach the popup to the marker
      .addTo(map);                   // finally add it to the map

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  Open the popup when the user clicks the marker
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // The popup is automatically opened on a click of the marker
    // when you call `setPopup()`.  If you want to control it yourself,
    // you can listen for the click on the marker’s DOM element:
    marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', () => {
      popup.addTo(map);               // ensures the popup is attached
      popup.setLngLat(marker.getLngLat()); // keep it anchored to the pin
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣  (Optional) Update the popup text after a drag
    // -------------------------------------------------
    marker.on('dragend', () => {
      const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
      // Example: show the new coordinates in the popup
      popup.setHTML(
        `<strong>New position</strong><br/>` +
        `lon: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br/>` +
        `lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`
      );
      // Keep the popup anchored to the marker after the drag
      popup.setLngLat(lngLat);
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## How It Works – Step-by-Step

| Step | What it does | Key API |
|------|--------------|----------|
| **1** | Initialise the map with a container, style, centre, and zoom. | `new maplibregl.Map()` |
| **2** | Wait for the `load` event so the style (including sprite sheets) is ready before we add UI elements. | `map.on('load', …)` |
| **3** | Create a `Popup` instance. It’s not shown yet; we’ll open it on click. | `new maplibregl.Popup()` |
| **4** | Create a `Marker` with `draggable: true`, set its initial coordinate, attach the popup, and add it to the map. | `new maplibregl.Marker()`, `.setLngLat()`, `.setPopup()`, `.addTo()` |
| **5** | The popup automatically opens when the marker is clicked because we called `setPopup()`. If you need custom logic, you can listen to the marker’s DOM element (`marker.getElement()`) and call `popup.addTo(map)`. | `marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', …)` |
| **6** *(optional)* | After a drag finishes (`dragend`), we read the new coordinates (`marker.getLngLat()`) and update the popup’s HTML. We also re-anchor the popup to the new location. | `marker.on('dragend', …)`, `popup.setHTML()`, `popup.setLngLat()` |

### Why Use a `Marker` + `Popup`?

* **Marker** – a DOM element positioned on the map canvas. It lives **outside** the style, so it never collides with other symbols and is always drawn on top.
* **draggable: true** – MapLibre handles the mouse/touch interaction for you; you only need to listen for `dragend` if you want to react to the new position.
* **Popup** – a small floating box that can contain any HTML. When you call `marker.setPopup(popup)`, MapLibre automatically opens the popup on a click of the marker (and closes it when the map is clicked elsewhere, unless you set `closeOnClick: false`).

### Customising the Pin

If you don’t like the default blue-pin image, you can supply your own HTML element:

```js
const el = document.createElement('div');
el.style.width = '30px';
el.style.height = '30px';
el.style.background = 'url(https://example.com/my-pin.svg) center / contain no-repeat';
el.style.cursor = 'pointer';

const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ element: el, draggable: true })
  .setLngLat([lon, lat])
  .setPopup(popup)
  .addTo(map);
```

The element can be any DOM node (e.g., an `<img>` or a `<div>` with CSS). The rest of the logic stays the same.

### Common Pitfalls

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Popup never appears on click | `setPopup()` not called or the marker’s element is covered by another overlay. | Ensure `marker.setPopup(popup)` is executed **after** the marker is created and before `addTo(map)`. |
| Marker doesn’t move when dragged | `draggable: true` omitted or CSS `pointer-events: none` on the custom element. | Add `draggable: true` and make sure the element can receive pointer events. |
| Coordinates are wrong after drag | Using `event.lngLat` from a `click` listener instead of `marker.getLngLat()` after drag. | In `dragend`, call `marker.getLngLat()` to read the final position. |
| Popup stays at the old location after drag | Popup not re-anchored. | Call `popup.setLngLat(marker.getLngLat())` inside the `dragend` handler. |

---

### TL;DR Code Snippet (minimal)

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ offset: 25 })
    .setHTML('Click the pin');

  const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable: true })
    .setLngLat([-122.42, 37.77])
    .setPopup(popup)          // click → popup opens
    .addTo(map);

  // Keep popup text in sync after a drag
  marker.on('dragend', () => {
    const pos = marker.getLngLat();
    popup.setHTML(`lon: ${pos.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>lat: ${pos.lat.toFixed(5)}`);
    popup.setLngLat(pos);
  });
});
```

That’s all you need to place a draggable pin on a MapLibre GL JS map and show a popup when the user clicks it. Happy mapping!
